[TEXT] The startup script is the smoking gun. The iptables rules are **statically applied at boot** via ACL translation from Cisco syntax (`ip access-group 110 in` on eth2). The critical detail: **all rules have `-i eth2`** (inbound on WAN link). This means:

- Traffic FROM pc15 → outbound via eth1→eth2: **NOT blocked** (rules only match `-i eth2`)
- Traffic FROM other sites → reply enters eth2: **BLOCKED** (rules match, DROP TCP/UDP/all)

This creates **asymmetric one-way black hole**: outbound from pc15 works, replies are dropped. TCP connections start (SYN sent) but SYN-ACK never arrives — explaining the user's "moody/intermittent" perception (connections appear to initiate then stall). No tc anomalies (default pfifo_fast on all interfaces). No periodic scripts. Root cause confirmed.

