[FINAL-TEXT] The startup script is the smoking gun. The iptables rules are **statically applied at boot** via ACL translation from Cisco syntax (`ip access-group 110 in` on eth2). The critical detail: **all rules have `-i eth2`** (inbound on WAN link). This means:

- Traffic FROM pc15 → outbound via eth1→eth2: **NOT blocked** (rules only match `-i eth2`)
- Traffic FROM other sites → reply enters eth2: **BLOCKED** (rules match, DROP TCP/UDP/all)

This creates **asymmetric one-way black hole**: outbound from pc15 works, replies are dropped. TCP connections start (SYN sent) but SYN-ACK never arrives — explaining the user's "moody/intermittent" perception (connections appear to initiate then stall). No tc anomalies (default pfifo_fast on all interfaces). No periodic scripts. Root cause confirmed.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component**: iptables FORWARD chain on router6, translated from a Cisco `ip access-group 110 in` applied inbound on the WAN interface (eth2 / Serial2/0, 10.50.1.2/24).

**Mechanism**: The startup script installs four FORWARD chain rules all scoped to `-i eth2` (traffic arriving on the WAN link from router3): (1) ACCEPT TCP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 port 80 only; (2) DROP all TCP; (3) DROP all UDP; (4) DROP everything else. Because rules only match on inbound interface eth2, outbound packets from pc15-18 (which enter router6 on eth0/eth1 and leave via eth2) are *not* filtered — they escape normally. But every reply from remote sites arrives back on eth2 and hits the DROP rules before it can be forwarded to pc15-18. The result is a one-way black hole: pc15 can send packets to 192.168.200.x or 172.16.10.x, but the ICMP/TCP/UDP replies are silently dropped on re-entry. TCP connections appear to initiate (SYN leaves) but stall waiting for SYN-ACK, producing the "there one minute, gone the next" intermittent experience reported by the user.

**Localization**: router6, iptables FORWARD chain, rules keyed on `-i eth2`. RIP (ripd, locally terminated on INPUT chain) and pc15-18 gateway reachability (router6 eth1 local) are unaffected, confirming the fault is exclusively in the forwarding path for inter-site transit traffic.

**Root cause**: An over-restrictive Cisco ACL has been translated into iptables rules on router6's WAN-facing interface that block all return traffic from remote sites to the 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 subnets.

**Fix**: Remove the DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain (`iptables -F FORWARD`) and replace with a policy that permits bidirectional inter-site forwarding.
=== END ===